# Auth module requests.
#
# In VS Code with the httpyac extension, each block gets a "Send Request" link.
# Headlessly:
#
#   pnpm exec httpyac send apps/api/http/auth.http --all
#   pnpm exec httpyac send apps/api/http/auth.http --name login
#
# Requires the API running (pnpm --filter api start:dev) and Postgres up.
#
# The password below is a local test fixture, not a secret. If these are ever
# pointed at a deployed environment, move it into a .env file httpyac reads
# rather than editing it here.

@host = http://localhost:3000
@email = ada@example.com
@password = correct-horse-battery
@id = d7190d21-01fb-4bee-983f-0606f4544118

###
# Create the account. 201, and the body must NOT contain passwordHash.
# Running this twice is itself a test - see the next block.
# @name register
POST {{host}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# Same email again -> 409. Uniqueness is enforced by the Postgres index from
# 1.2b, surfaced by catching P2002 - not by a check-then-act read.
POST {{host}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# Too short -> 400, listing the constraint. Rejected by the ValidationPipe
# before the service runs, so nothing is hashed and nothing is written.
POST {{host}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "grace@example.com",
  "password": "short"
}

###
# A smuggled field -> 201, with isAdmin silently stripped by whitelist: true.
# Check the users table afterwards: no trace of it anywhere.
POST {{host}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "hopper@example.com",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "isAdmin": true
}

###
# Log in. 200, not 201 - nothing was created.
#
# @name captures the response so later requests can use {{login.accessToken}}
# instead of you copying tokens around. That pays off in 1.4c, when there is
# finally a route that demands one.
#
# @jwt decodes the token in the output, so you can read your own sub and exp.
# If httpyac complains about that line, delete it - it is a convenience, not
# part of the test.
# @name login
# @jwt accessToken
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# Wrong password -> 401.
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "definitely-not-the-password"
}

###
# Email that does not exist -> 401.
#
# This response must be byte-for-byte identical to the one above. Same status,
# same message. If you can tell them apart, the API is telling an attacker
# which addresses are registered.
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@example.com",
  "password": "definitely-not-the-password"
}

###
# The current user. 200, with the id and email of whoever logged in above.
#
# Note what this request does NOT contain: no id in the URL, no body. The token
# is the identity. Sending an id would let any caller read any account.
#
# {{login.accessToken}} comes from the named login request above - that capture
# is the whole reason for naming it, and why this is not a copy-paste job.
# @name me
GET {{host}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{login.accessToken}}

###
# The same request with no Authorization header -> 401.
#
# This is the one that proves the guard is guarding. A passing /auth/me on its
# own only shows the happy path works; it says nothing about whether the route
# is actually protected.
GET {{host}}/auth/me


### 
# Refresh the access token. 200, with a new access token and the same refresh
# token. The new access token is valid for 15 minutes, the refresh token for
# 7 days. The refresh token is stored in the database, so it can be revoked
# if the user logs out or the token is compromised.
#
# @name refresh
POST {{host}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json  

{
  "refreshToken": "iJg9SGdor7zxUmTdDRXZxFgKRDOZexvHhpk2M-YdosM"
}
